{"name":"Foxflower Viola","hex":"#a2acc5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"viola","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several flowering plants, of the genus Viola, including the violets and pansies."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bowed  tenor instrument larger than a violin but smaller than a cello, used in orchestras and string quartets.","An organ stop with a similar tone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/viola"}],"entries":[{"title":"Viola","blocks":[{"text":"The viola ( vee-OH-lə, Italian: [ˈvjɔːla, viˈɔːla]) is a string instrument of the violin family, and is usually bowed when played. The viola is slightly larger than the violin and has a lower sound. Since the 18th century, it has been the middle or alto voice of the violin family, between the violin (which is tuned a perfect fifth higher) and the cello (which is tuned an octave lower). The strings from low to high are typically tuned to C3, G3, D4, and A4."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Bratsche.jpg/330px-Bratsche.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viola","wikidata":"Q80284"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Range_viola.png/500px-Range_viola.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Range_viola.png/500px-Range_viola.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARange_viola.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Viola_Bridge.jpg/500px-Viola_Bridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Viola_Bridge.jpg/1280px-Viola_Bridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conte